%U A399272 11,2,3,4,6,7,8,9,10,12,5,6,8,9,10,13,12,11,2,3,4,7,1,5
%N A399272 Triangle T(n,k) read by rows that has the A001710(n)-th permutation of [1,...,n] as row n, using Heap's method as permutation generator.
%C A399272 Put simply, the rows represent the states when the task of generating all permutations of [1,...,n] is halfway complete.
%H A399272 Hugo Pfoertner, <a href="/A399272/b399272.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..136</a>, rows 1..16, flattened.
%H A399272 B. R. Heap, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/6.3.293">Permutations by Interchanges</a>, The Computer Journal, Volume 6, Issue 3, November 1963, Pages 293-298.
%H A399272 Robert Sedgewick, <a href="https://sedgewick.io/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/2002PermGeneration.pdf">Permutation Generation Methods</a>, talk on 4 June 2020. (see page 16, Implementation of Heap’s method (nonrecursive)).
%H A399272 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heap%27s_algorithm">Heap's algorithm</a>
%e A399272 The triangle begins:
%e A399272    1,
%e A399272    1, 2,
%e A399272    3, 1, 2,
%e A399272    1, 2, 4, 3,
%e A399272    2, 4, 5, 1, 3,
%e A399272    6, 5, 3, 4, 1, 2,
%e A399272    4, 7, 6, 5, 3, 1, 2,
%e A399272    1, 7, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 3,
%e A399272    4, 6, 9, 8, 7, 2, 5, 1, 3,
%e A399272   10, 9, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 1, 4,
%e A399272   ...
%Y A399272 Cf. A280318.
%Y A399272 Cf. A375300, A398687 (similar, but for lexicographic and reverse colex order).
